<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  OBD Platform - Phase B live FreeSWITCH environment
  Sofia "internal" SIP profile.

  Replaces the stock internal profile (22 KB, mostly documentation) with an
  explicit minimal profile for a private development switch.

  Role in the Java contract: softphone registration and local SIP endpoint
  testing. The Java platform never dials this profile directly - it dials
  sofia/gateway/<gateway>/<destination> - but every agent/callee leg and
  every inbound channel terminates on a profile like this one.

  Deliberate omissions and why:
    - sip-ip / rtp-ip are left unset. mod_sofia then binds the SIP
      listener to all interfaces (reachable from the Docker network and
      from the published 127.0.0.1 ports) while still auto-detecting a
      real address for the SDP connection line. Pinning rtp-ip to
      0.0.0.0 would advertise 0.0.0.0 in SDP and break media.
    - All TLS parameters (tls, tls-sip-port, tls-cert-dir, ...) are
      omitted, so no TLS listener is bound.
    - ws-binding / wss-binding are omitted, so 5066 and 7443 are NOT
      bound. The stock internal profile binds both.
    - Presence, recording, watchdog and challenge-realm tuning are omitted.
-->
<!--
  Root element note: a sofia profile file must have <profile name="..."> as
  its root element, exactly as the stock sip_profiles/*.xml files do. A
  <configuration name="sofia.conf"> root is silently ignored by mod_sofia -
  the profile is compiled into the configuration but never instantiated.
-->
<profile name="internal">

  <settings>
    <param name="debug" value="0"/>
    <param name="sip-trace" value="no"/>
    <param name="sip-capture" value="no"/>

    <param name="sip-port" value="5060"/>
    <param name="dialplan" value="XML"/>
    <param name="context" value="public"/>

    <!-- === Codecs ===
         PCMU/PCMA first: the deterministic, universally supported pair
         for RTP/DTMF testing. G722 and OPUS follow.
         rfc2833-pt is 101, matching the stock profiles. -->
    <param name="inbound-codec-prefs" value="PCMU,PCMA,G722,OPUS"/>
    <param name="outbound-codec-prefs" value="PCMU,PCMA,G722,OPUS"/>
    <param name="rfc2833-pt" value="101"/>
    <param name="inbound-codec-negotiation" value="generous"/>

    <!-- === DTMF (Phase B: preparation only, no collection is implemented) ===

         liberal-dtmf: accept DTMF as either RFC 2833/4733 or SIP INFO
         without requiring the endpoint to have signalled a matching
         method. Stock default is false, which silently drops one of the
         two. Required so that real DTMF can later reach the Java platform
         as CHANNEL_DTMF from any endpoint type.

         proxy-info: relay inbound SIP INFO across a bridge instead of
         consuming it locally. Required for CONNECT_BY_AGENT, where the
         agent's keys must survive the bridge. Stock default is false.

         NOTE: no FreeSWITCH-side digit collection is configured - no
         play_and_collect_digits, no read, no start_dtmf. The Java platform
         owns DTMF collection and only consumes CHANNEL_DTMF events. -->
    <param name="liberal-dtmf" value="true"/>
    <param name="proxy-info" value="true"/>
    <param name="dtmf-duration" value="2000"/>

    <!-- === Security: private development network only === -->
    <param name="apply-inbound-acl" value="obd-dev-acl"/>
    <param name="local-network-acl" value="obd-dev-acl"/>
    <param name="auth-calls" value="false"/>

    <!--
      Registration authentication: ENABLED as of Phase C.

      Phase B set this false. That was the right call then - nothing registered,
      and the stock directory ships default_password=1234 (vars.xml), so
      enabling registration would have meant accepting a publicly known
      credential. Phase C needs REGISTER / 401 / digest / 200 OK to be observed,
      so registration is enabled now - but only because a dedicated test
      directory is mounted (directory/obd-test.xml) containing exactly two
      local extensions, 1001 and 1002, with strong per-extension secrets
      rendered from the environment at container start.

      It remains false on the external profile: that profile faces trunks, not
      local subscribers.
    -->
    <param name="auth-subscriptions" value="true"/>

    <!--
      Fixed authentication realm. PHASE C CHANGE - this fixes a real defect
      that Phase C discovered by observation.

      The stock value is "auto_to", which takes the realm from the To header,
      i.e. from whatever address the client happened to dial. That makes the
      identity of a registration depend on the network path used to reach
      FreeSWITCH, and it desynchronises two things that must agree:

        the realm FreeSWITCH authenticates against, and
        the key it files the registration under,  user@realm

      Observed in Phase C, host-based test client:

        REGISTER To: sip:1002@127.0.0.1   -> realm 127.0.0.1
        stored as                            1002@127.0.0.1
        but the stock dialplan then dialled   user/1002@172.25.0.2
        and FreeSWITCH answered:
          Cannot create outgoing channel of type [error]
          cause: [USER_NOT_REGISTERED]

      Pinning the realm to $${domain} makes authentication, the registration
      key and the dialplan's user lookup all agree on one value, so the same
      extension works whether it registers from the host or from inside the
      Docker network.

      $${domain} is a FreeSWITCH preprocessor variable, substituted at config
      compile time. It resolves to the container's own IPv4 address here.
    -->
    <param name="challenge-realm" value="$${domain}"/>

    <!-- === Media ===
         rtp-timeout-sec / rtp-hold-timeout-sec are deliberately NOT set:
         FreeSWITCH 1.11 honours them but logs a deprecation warning on
         every load, and the suggested replacements (media_timeout /
         media_hold_timeout) are channel variables, not profile parameters.
         The compiled defaults are the same 300 / 1800 the stock profiles
         set, so omitting them is behaviour-neutral and warning-free. -->
    <param name="rtp-timer-name" value="soft"/>
    <param name="inbound-late-negotiation" value="true"/>
    <param name="hold-music" value="$${hold_music}"/>
  </settings>

  <!--
    GATEWAYS on the INTERNAL profile (Phase E).

    Why the local test gateway is declared HERE and not on the external
    profile, which is where the carrier trunk lives. This was measured, not
    assumed, and the first attempt got it wrong.

    mod_sofia resolves gateways as:
        sofia.conf -> profiles -> profile[@name] -> gateways -> gateway

    so a gateway's ORIGINATION profile is the profile that DECLARES it. The
    gateway's own <param name="sofia-profile"> is a hint that the declaring
    profile overrides: with the local gateway declared on "external" and
    sofia-profile=internal, every origination still produced a channel named
    sofia/external/... and the parameter had no effect at all.

    The profile choice is also forced by WHERE THE ROUTE LIVES. The endpoint
    registers to THIS profile, so its contact lives in sofia_reg_internal, and
    mod_sofia resolves an outbound destination against the registration
    database of the profile it is originating from. A bare destination such as
    "1002" - which is exactly what the Java dial string supplies, as
    sofia/gateway/<gw>/1002 - can therefore only be resolved here.

    See ADR-007 and infra/freeswitch/gateway/local-endpoint.xml for the full
    measurement, including the direct-address forms that cannot route at all.

    "fs-gateway" is deliberately NOT included here. It stays on the external
    profile, because it is a carrier trunk and must not become resolvable
    from the internal subscriber network.
  -->
  <gateways>
    <X-PRE-PROCESS cmd="include" data="../gateway/local-endpoint.xml"/>
  </gateways>

</profile>
